<TD VALIGN=top>
<H2><FONT COLOR="#D60021">1.4.2.9.3.</FONT></H2>
</TD>
<TD VALIGN=top>
<H2>Weibull Analysis</H2>
</TD>
</TR >
</TABLE>
</TD>
</TR>







<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Plots for Weibull Distribution
</I>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
The following plots were generated for a
<a href="../section3/eda3668.htm">Weibull distribution</a>.
<p>
<IMG SRC="fuller/gif/weibppcc.gif"
     alt="plots generated for a Weibull distribution">
   
</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Conclusions
</I>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
We can make the following conclusions from these plots.
<ol>
   <li>The optimal value, in the sense of having the most linear
       probability plot, of the shape parameter gamma is 2.13.
       <p>
   <li>At the optimal value of the shape parameter, the PPCC
       value is 0.988.
       <p>
   <li>At the optimal value of the shape parameter, the estimate
       of the location parameter is 15.90 and the estimate of the
       scale parameter is 16.92.
       <p>
   <li>Fine tuning the estimate of gamma (from 2 to 2.13) has
       minimal impact on the PPCC value.
</ol>

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Alternative Plots
</I>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
The <a href="../section3/weibplot.htm">Weibull plot</a> and the
<a href="../../apr/section2/apr222.htm"> Weibull hazard plot</a>
are alternative graphical analysis procedures to the PPCC plots and
probability plots.
<p>
These two procedures, especially the Weibull plot, are very commonly
employed.  That not withstanding, the disadvantage of these two
procedures is that they both assume that the location parameter
(i.e., the lower bound) is zero and that we are fitting a 
2-parameter Weibull instead of a 3-parameter Weibull.  The
advantage is that there is an extensive literature on these methods
and they have been designed to work with either
<a href="../../apr/section1/apr131.htm">censored or
uncensored</a> data.

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<A NAME="WEIBPLOT">
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Weibull Plot
</I>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
<img src="fuller/gif/weibplot.gif" alt="Weibull Plot">
<p>
This Weibull plot shows the following
<ol>
   <li>The Weibull plot is approximately linear indicating that
       the 2-parameter Weibull provides an adequate fit to the
       data.
       <p>
   <li>The estimate of the shape parameter is 5.28 and the estimate
       of the scale parameter is 33.32.
</ol>

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<A NAME="WEIBHAZA">
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Weibull Hazard Plot
</I>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
<img src="fuller/gif/weibhaza.gif" alt="Weibull Hazard Plot">
<p>
The construction and interpretation of the Weibull hazard plot is
discussed in the <a href="../../apr/section2/apr222.htm">Assessing
Product Reliability</a> chapter.
<ol>
</ol>

</TD>
</TR>
